{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '50,150p'\"","aggregated_output":"    50\t\\paragraph{Evaluation discipline.}\n    51\tModel selection can overfit its own validation criterion \\citep{Cawley2010}, and repeated adaptive reuse of evaluation data requires care \\citep{Dwork2015}. We consequently separate development feedback from the recorded held-out comparison. Bootstrap uncertainty \\citep{Efron1979} is reported under the evaluator's actual resampling unit, not as a distribution-free certificate. None of these methodological connections turns the observed comparison into evidence of universal benchmark predictability.\n    52\t\n    53\t\\section{Method}\n    54\t\\subsection{Problem and Estimation Target}\n    55\tFor one benchmark, let $X\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M\\times N}$ contain source-model responses on $N$ retained item columns. A target model has response vector $y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}$ and full accuracy\n    56\t\\begin{equation}\n    57\ts(y)=\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=1}^{N}y_j.\n    58\t\\label{eq:target}\n    59\t\\end{equation}\n    60\tOnly responses on a subset $Q_k$, of size $k$, are made available to the estimator. The output is $\\hat s(y_{Q_k};X)\\in[0,1]$. Historical source responses can be used in fitting and tuning; unqueried target responses and full target scores cannot. The target is the mean over retained columns, not a claim to recover an unreconciled original benchmark or a differently weighted official leaderboard score.\n    61\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/episodes/0/checkpoints]]\n    62\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L844]]\n    63\t\n    64\tSource rows have nonnegative weights $w_m$ summing to unity. The supplied protocol gives each metadata-defined source lineage equal total weight and distributes that weight equally among its rows. Item-content groups constrain selection: at most one item from any group is queried. However, all retained columns, including their multiplicities, remain in Equation~\\ref{eq:target}. Thus query uniqueness and score weighting are distinct operations. The same nested path $Q_5\\subset Q_{10}\\subset Q_{25}$ serves all targets within a fitted case.\n    65\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L556]]\n    66\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L99]]\n    67\t\n    68\t\\subsection{Score-Directed Shared Acquisition}\n    69\tDefine the weighted item means $\\mu_j=\\sum_m w_mX_{mj}$, source full scores $s_m=N^{-1}\\sum_jX_{mj}$, and weighted score mean $\\bar s=\\sum_mw_ms_m$. Let\n    70\t\\begin{equation}\n    71\tZ_{mj}=\\sqrt{w_m}(X_{mj}-\\mu_j),\\quad\n    72\tu_m=\\sqrt{w_m}(s_m-\\bar s),\\quad C=Z^\\top Z,\\quad c=Z^\\top u.\n    73\t\\label{eq:covariance}\n    74\t\\end{equation}\n    75\tThe vector $c$ targets full accuracy explicitly. A high item variance alone does not imply a high score covariance; a high marginal covariance can also become redundant after other items are selected.\n    76\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L6]]\n    77\t\n    78\tFor regularization $\\lambda>0$, a partial subset $Q$ has regularized score information $c_Q^\\top(C_{QQ}+\\lambda I)^{-1}c_Q$. A Schur-complement expansion yields the incremental criterion\n    79\t\\begin{equation}\n    80\tg(j\\mid Q)=\n    81\t\\frac{\\left[c_j-C_{jQ}(C_{QQ}+\\lambda I)^{-1}c_Q\\right]^2}\n    82\t{C_{jj}+\\lambda-C_{jQ}(C_{QQ}+\\lambda I)^{-1}C_{Qj}}.\n    83\t\\label{eq:gain}\n    84\t\\end{equation}\n    85\tThe readable selector greedily adds the legal item maximizing this quantity, masking every item in an already queried content group. Empty-set terms vanish. This construction optimizes a source empirical linear-prediction surrogate; it neither guarantees target MAE reduction nor proves that the greedy subset is globally optimal. Ridge regularization also changes the acquisition ranking, not merely the final regression fit.\n    86\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L31]]\n    87\t\n    88\tThe rationale is to prefer responses that explain residual variation in the quantity actually being estimated. In contrast, response-space coverage can emphasize diversity without accounting for whether that diversity changes the full mean. This is a design hypothesis: the available comparison changes the decoder as well as the query path, so it cannot establish that Equation~\\ref{eq:gain} alone explains the measured advantage.\n    89\t\n    90\t\\subsection{All-Observation Score Decoding}\n    91\tFor observed history $z=y_Q$, the linear decoder uses\n    92\t\\begin{align}\n    93\t\\beta_Q&=(C_{QQ}+\\lambda I)^{-1}c_Q,\\\\\n    94\t\\hat s_{\\mathrm{ridge}}(z)&=\\bar s+(z-\\mu_Q)^\\top\\beta_Q,\\qquad\n    95\t\\hat s_{\\mathrm{offset}}(z)=\\bar s+\\frac{1}{|Q|}\\sum_{j\\in Q}(z_j-\\mu_j),\\\\\n    96\t\\hat s_L(z)&=\\clip\\left[\\alpha\\hat s_{\\mathrm{ridge}}(z)+(1-\\alpha)\\hat s_{\\mathrm{offset}}(z)\\right].\n    97\t\\label{eq:linear}\n    98\t\\end{align}\n    99\tThe offset term corrects the source score mean using the target's average deviation from source item means. Every queried answer contributes to decoding; identical answer histories map to the same prediction. With no observations, the fallback is the source score mean. Clipping bounds predictions but does not supply confidence intervals or unbiasedness.\n   100\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L75]]\n   101\t\n   102\tThe supplied implementation also admits an optional decoder that infers a regularized scalar log-odds shift from queried answers, averages its predicted probabilities over retained items, and adds a source-fitted residual ridge correction. It can blend this estimate with Equation~\\ref{eq:linear}, including a purely linear fallback. Its residual features and fitted shifts use the same query prefix during training and inference. Supplementary Information gives the equations. Since no per-case branch selections or component ablations are attested, we do not label the measured gain a nonlinear-decoder effect.\n   103\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18]]\n   104\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L233]]\n   105\t\n   106\t\\subsection{Source-Only Selection of Settings}\n   107\tSettings are selected with lineage-disjoint source folds. Within each fold, item statistics and the acquisition path are refitted on fitting rows, and accuracy estimates are assessed on held-out source lineages. Loss averages absolute errors over the evaluation prefixes, then gives held-out lineages equal weight. The optional decoder adds an outer source split that refits the linear selector and its tuning within the fitting partition. No target full score is supplied for these choices.\n   108\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L111]]\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L142]]\n   110\t\n   111\tThe source counterpart is inspectable, but the retained final source-tree digest does not itself prove byte identity with that available tree. We therefore distinguish an implementation-grounded description of the supplied counterpart from the directly attested final predictions. This boundary is particularly important for settings or conditional branches absent from the measurement records. The empirical conclusions below require the recorded predictions, not an assumption about which optional branch produced them.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/inventory.json]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/program_tree_sha256]]\n   114\t\n   115\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n   116\t\\subsection{Data, Splits, and Budget Matching}\n   117\tThe recorded final evaluation covers nine binary benchmark matrices. The leaderboard-derived group contains BBH \\citep{Suzgun2022}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023}, MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023}, and a MATH subset \\citep{Hendrycks2021}. The HELM-derived group \\citep{Liang2023} contains LegalBench \\citep{Guha2023}, MATH, MedQA \\citep{Jin2020}, and MMLU \\citep{Hendrycks2020}. These are the supplied retained matrices, not newly collected LLM outputs. Distinguishing leaderboard MATH from HELM MATH is essential because they are separate evaluation cases.\n   118\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   119\t\n   120\tDevelopment feedback concerned ARC-Challenge, IFEval, Commonsense, and GSM matrices; it is not pooled with the final metrics. The frozen package is refitted from the source rows of each final benchmark. Hence this is evaluation across held-out benchmark cases with per-benchmark source fitting, not transfer of a single fixed set of coefficients across tasks. No additional evaluated task family is supplied beyond this benchmark-prediction setting.\n   121\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   122\t\n   123\tThe frozen resource manifests describe model-lineage separation and conservative item-content reconciliation across development and final data. Anonymous group identifiers, rather than model names or item text, reach the estimator. Known aliases and declared base-model relations guide grouping; undocumented derivatives can remain related across partitions. Common-stem normalization likewise does not establish semantic or pretraining disjointness. These are metadata-based safeguards, not proof of independent source and target populations.\n   124\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/bbh_manifest.json#/lineage_policy]]\n   125\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/manifests/bbh_manifest.json#/limitations]]\n   126\t\n   127\tEvaluation uses seeds 1009, 2003, and 3001 and cumulative query budgets 5, 10, and 25. This yields 81 non-replay benchmark--budget--seed cells. Each reset discards fitted state. A repeated normal BBH record checks deterministic replay but is excluded from performance averages. Identical-history and duplicated-target checks assess prediction consistency; additional perturbed observations are reported separately, not counted as ordinary performance measurements.\n   128\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/cell_count]]\n   129\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   130\t\n   131\t\\subsection{Compared Estimators}\n   132\tAll nonzero-cost estimators use the same reported item budget, but not necessarily the same items or fitting cost. The \\textbf{source prior} returns the weighted source mean and uses no target answers. \\textbf{Uniform sampling} selects a random prefix of content-group representatives and scales observed outcomes by each group's retained-column mass. Its clipped, group-mass-weighted estimate is not simply an unweighted mean when group sizes differ. \\textbf{Local anchors} constructs normalized source response embeddings, chooses representatives by a farthest-point rule, assigns all retained items to selected representatives, and uses assignment masses to weight their target outcomes. This is a local anchor estimator rather than an exact published Anchor Points reproduction.\n   133\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L580]]\n   134\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L598]]\n   135\t\n   136\t\\textbf{Local IRT} uses a logistic ability--difficulty model with regularized ability fitting. It shares the uniform baseline's item path, estimates target ability from the queried answers, and averages predicted probabilities across retained items. Its source-fitted difficulties and implementation details are specified in Supplementary Information. The \\textbf{score-targeted package} changes both selection and decoding. Accordingly its comparison with IRT is a package comparison at matched observation budgets, not a selector-only or decoder-only ablation. None of the local comparators establishes a state-of-the-art comparison with the full published systems.\n   137\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L636]]\n   138\t\n   139\t\\subsection{Metrics and Conditional Uncertainty}\n   140\tWithin each cell, each target lineage receives equal total weight, distributed equally among its models. MAE is the resulting weighted absolute error, and RMSE is the square root of weighted squared error. Ranking metrics are Spearman correlation and Kendall's $\\tau_b$ between lineage-mean predictions and lineage-mean true scores, not correlations over individual rows. Aggregate metrics average cells equally, giving each benchmark, budget, and seed equal weight. In particular, aggregate RMSE is a mean of cell RMSEs, not a pooled squared-error statistic.\n   141\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L556]]\n   142\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/aggregate]]\n   143\t\n   144\tThe evaluator supplies paired percentile intervals from 200 lineage-block bootstrap draws. Within a benchmark, lineage blocks retain their predictions across budgets and seeds; benchmarks retain equal weights. The same aggregate uncertainty object is repeated in the outer seed records and is used only once. These intervals condition on the fixed cases and stored fitting outcomes. They do not resample benchmarks, refit source data, quantify future-family transfer, or provide individual-target prediction intervals. They are the recorded percentile procedure, not higher-order corrected intervals \\citep{Efron1987}. No failed normal cells are recorded. The evaluator's completion and qualification outcome are both affirmative; that procedural result does not certify scientific generality.\n   145\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/uncertainty]]\n   146\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   147\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   148\t\n   149\t\\section{Results}\n   150\t\\subsection{Average Accuracy and Ranking}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '36,52p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '98,121p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '240,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '36,52p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '98,121p'; nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '240,290p'\"","aggregated_output":"    36\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth,height=2in,keepaspectratio]{figures/score_prediction_overview.png}\n    37\t\\caption{Estimation setting and information boundary. Source response matrices determine a shared score-directed item path and source-selected decoder. Only the target responses at the queried items enter prediction; retained full-benchmark target scores are used for evaluation. The optional decoder branch is not independently attested per case.}\n    38\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    39\t\\end{figure}\n    40\t\n    41\t\\section{Related Work}\n    42\t\\paragraph{Compact benchmark evaluation.}\n    43\tAnchor Points uses correlations in model responses to construct small representative evaluation sets \\citep{Vivek2024}. tinyBenchmarks studies curated subsets and score estimation across established evaluation suites \\citep{Polo2024}; metabench compresses reasoning and knowledge tests using informative items and ability estimates \\citep{Kipnis2025}. These works motivate source-response reuse, but their reported results are not experimental baselines here. Our anchor and item-response comparators are the specific local implementations described below, not reproductions of every feature of those systems.\n    44\t\n    45\tSupervised assessors can predict a new model's performance from its responses on reference instances and features of the instance to be predicted \\citep{Pacchiardi2024}. Our setting instead predicts the aggregate score directly from binary response histories, without using item-text features. Learned acquisition policies offer another route to efficient evaluation \\citep{Li2025}. In contrast, the item path considered here is fixed from source data before target responses arrive. Adaptive evaluation with continuous bounded scores \\citep{Balkir2026} addresses a broader observation model and stopping problem; our evidence is restricted to fixed-budget binary matrices. ONEBench studies aggregation over heterogeneous, incomplete evaluations \\citep{Ghosh2024}, whereas the present target is a retained benchmark's arithmetic mean.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\paragraph{Targeted design and regularization.}\n    48\tSelecting measurements for a particular linear functional, rather than estimating all parameters equally well, is a classical experimental-design distinction \\citep{Elfving1952}. The score-directed criterion below is an empirical, regularized covariance construction, not a claim to solve that general design problem optimally. Ridge regularization stabilizes regression when observations are correlated \\citep{Hoerl1970}. Here both the acquisition criterion and decoder depend on source-derived covariance, making source-to-target stability an empirical assumption rather than a consequence of regularization.\n    49\t\n    50\t\\paragraph{Evaluation discipline.}\n    51\tModel selection can overfit its own validation criterion \\citep{Cawley2010}, and repeated adaptive reuse of evaluation data requires care \\citep{Dwork2015}. We consequently separate development feedback from the recorded held-out comparison. Bootstrap uncertainty \\citep{Efron1979} is reported under the evaluator's actual resampling unit, not as a distribution-free certificate. None of these methodological connections turns the observed comparison into evidence of universal benchmark predictability.\n    52\t\n    98\t\\end{align}\n    99\tThe offset term corrects the source score mean using the target's average deviation from source item means. Every queried answer contributes to decoding; identical answer histories map to the same prediction. With no observations, the fallback is the source score mean. Clipping bounds predictions but does not supply confidence intervals or unbiasedness.\n   100\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L75]]\n   101\t\n   102\tThe supplied implementation also admits an optional decoder that infers a regularized scalar log-odds shift from queried answers, averages its predicted probabilities over retained items, and adds a source-fitted residual ridge correction. It can blend this estimate with Equation~\\ref{eq:linear}, including a purely linear fallback. Its residual features and fitted shifts use the same query prefix during training and inference. Supplementary Information gives the equations. Since no per-case branch selections or component ablations are attested, we do not label the measured gain a nonlinear-decoder effect.\n   103\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18]]\n   104\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L233]]\n   105\t\n   106\t\\subsection{Source-Only Selection of Settings}\n   107\tSettings are selected with lineage-disjoint source folds. Within each fold, item statistics and the acquisition path are refitted on fitting rows, and accuracy estimates are assessed on held-out source lineages. Loss averages absolute errors over the evaluation prefixes, then gives held-out lineages equal weight. The optional decoder adds an outer source split that refits the linear selector and its tuning within the fitting partition. No target full score is supplied for these choices.\n   108\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L111]]\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L142]]\n   110\t\n   111\tThe source counterpart is inspectable, but the retained final source-tree digest does not itself prove byte identity with that available tree. We therefore distinguish an implementation-grounded description of the supplied counterpart from the directly attested final predictions. This boundary is particularly important for settings or conditional branches absent from the measurement records. The empirical conclusions below require the recorded predictions, not an assumption about which optional branch produced them.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/inventory.json]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/program_tree_sha256]]\n   114\t\n   115\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n   116\t\\subsection{Data, Splits, and Budget Matching}\n   117\tThe recorded final evaluation covers nine binary benchmark matrices. The leaderboard-derived group contains BBH \\citep{Suzgun2022}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023}, MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023}, and a MATH subset \\citep{Hendrycks2021}. The HELM-derived group \\citep{Liang2023} contains LegalBench \\citep{Guha2023}, MATH, MedQA \\citep{Jin2020}, and MMLU \\citep{Hendrycks2020}. These are the supplied retained matrices, not newly collected LLM outputs. Distinguishing leaderboard MATH from HELM MATH is essential because they are separate evaluation cases.\n   118\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   119\t\n   120\tDevelopment feedback concerned ARC-Challenge, IFEval, Commonsense, and GSM matrices; it is not pooled with the final metrics. The frozen package is refitted from the source rows of each final benchmark. Hence this is evaluation across held-out benchmark cases with per-benchmark source fitting, not transfer of a single fixed set of coefficients across tasks. No additional evaluated task family is supplied beyond this benchmark-prediction setting.\n   121\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   240\t\\appendix\n   241\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   242\t\n   243\t\\subsection{Optional Decoder and Prefix-Matched Residuals}\n   244\tThe supplied readable counterpart constructs item logits $\\ell_j=\\log(\\tilde\\mu_j/(1-\\tilde\\mu_j))$, with item means clipped to $[10^{-4},1-10^{-4}]$. For a queried response vector $z$, it infers a scalar shift\n   245\t\\begin{equation}\n   246\t\\delta(z)=\\arg\\min_d\\left\\{\\sum_{j\\in Q}\\left[\\log(1+e^{\\ell_j+d})-z_j(\\ell_j+d)\\right]+\\frac{\\eta}{2}d^2\\right\\}.\n   247\t\\end{equation}\n   248\tIt predicts a full-score curve $f(d)=N^{-1}\\sum_j\\sigmoid(\\ell_j+d)$. For every source row, the shift is inferred using only its queried prefix, not its full response vector. Define query residuals $r_{mj}=X_{mj}-\\sigmoid(\\ell_j+\\delta(X_{mQ}))$ and score residuals $t_m=s_m-f(\\delta(X_{mQ}))$. Weighted centering gives $\\bar r,\\bar t$, and residual ridge coefficients\n   249\t\\begin{equation}\n   250\tb=(R_c^\\top W R_c+\\lambda_r I)^{-1}R_c^\\top W(t-\\bar t).\n   251\t\\end{equation}\n   252\tThe target estimate is $f(\\delta(z))+\\bar t+(r(z)-\\bar r)^\\top b$, blended with the linear decoder and clipped. This residual term is supervised score correction, not a randomized audit or a finite-population unbiasedness correction.\n   253\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18]]\n   254\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L65]]\n   255\t\n   256\tThe evaluator requests a fitting allowance of 200 and a settings cap of 4; these are interface allowances, not attested optimization-work counters. In the readable counterpart, the linear candidate list under that cap is $(\\lambda,\\alpha)\\in\\{(0.05,1),(0.01,1),(0.2,1),(0.05,0.5)\\}$. The optional decoder's capped list comprises the linear fallback and $(\\eta,\\lambda_r,\\gamma)\\in\\{(1,0.05,0.5),(1,0.05,1),(0.1,0.05,0.5)\\}$, where $\\gamma$ is the nonlinear blend weight. The wider uncapped list in the source is not the evaluated allowance. Source validation uses two folds and the 5/10/25 prefixes. Shift fitting uses a bounded Newton/bisection update, with at most 64 iterations and shifts bounded to $[-20,20]$. These details describe the source counterpart, not evidence that any particular setting was selected in a final case.\n   257\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L930]]\n   258\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/surrogate.py#L111]]\n   259\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L18]]\n   260\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/nonlinear_decoder.py#L129]]\n   261\t\n   262\t\\subsection{Local Baseline Definitions}\n   263\tLet $G$ be the number of item-content groups and $a_g$ the fraction of retained columns in group $g$. For a uniform sample of distinct group representatives, the implemented estimate is $\\clip[(G/k)\\sum_{j\\in Q_k}a_{g(j)}y_j]$. Unequal group masses can therefore produce estimates outside the unit interval before clipping. The source prior is $\\bar s$, independent of the target observations. Anchor embeddings center each item response vector using its weighted source item mean, multiply coordinates by $\\sqrt{w_m}$, and normalize their lengths. Selection starts near the embedding mean, proceeds by farthest-point coverage among group representatives, and uses maximum inner product for assigning all columns to selected anchors. Assignment frequencies determine prediction weights.\n   264\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L580]]\n   265\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L653]]\n   266\t\n   267\tLocal IRT initializes difficulty from clipped weighted item means in $[0.005,0.995]$ and performs 200 alternating source updates of ability and difficulty with logistic probabilities and regularization. The ability origin is recentered by its weighted source mean. Target fitting holds difficulties fixed and uses 40 regularized ability updates from the uniformly queried items. Target prediction averages logistic probabilities over the retained item population. The source means inform initialization, but the fitted difficulty vector is not merely the negative initial logit after the source updates.\n   268\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#L636]]\n   269\t\n   270\t\\subsection{Complete Benchmark--Budget Accuracy Results}\n   271\tTable~\\ref{tab:cases} gives seed-averaged errors for every final benchmark and budget, including all unfavorable package comparisons with IRT. The accompanying machine-readable file contains each seed separately and all reconstructed metrics. No absent cell is treated as a measured loss; the retained final records contain no failed normal cells. The protocol specifies fixed worst-case contributions for unavailable evaluations, but that rule is not invoked by this table.\n   272\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/by_case_budget]]\n   273\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/uncertainty/failed_normal_cells]]\n   274\t\n   275\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n   276\t\\centering\n   277\t\\caption{MAE in percentage points, averaged over the recorded seeds. All cases and budgets are retained.}\n   278\t\\label{tab:cases}\n   279\t% BEGIN GENERATED case_budget\n   280\t% @evidence[[analysis/paper_statistics.json#/by_case_budget]]\n   281\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrr}\n   282\t\\toprule\n   283\tBenchmark & Items & Package & IRT & Uniform & Prior & Anchors\\\\\n   284\t\\midrule\n   285\tBBH & 5 & 3.69 & 9.16 & 19.07 & 9.98 & 20.16 \\\\\n   286\tBBH & 10 & 2.72 & 7.13 & 12.66 & 9.98 & 19.05 \\\\\n   287\tBBH & 25 & 2.18 & 5.10 & 6.85 & 9.98 & 15.52 \\\\\n   288\tGPQA & 5 & 1.80 & 7.07 & 14.75 & 3.03 & 15.45 \\\\\n   289\tGPQA & 10 & 1.68 & 6.15 & 9.89 & 3.03 & 10.32 \\\\\n   290\tGPQA & 25 & 1.26 & 5.73 & 7.20 & 3.03 & 6.60 \\\\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
