{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat paper_strategy.json; sed -n '1,26p' global_memory_summary.md; head -n 60 claims_grounding.json; head -n 70 references_provenance.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Score-Targeted Subsets for Predicting LLM Benchmark Accuracy\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a source-trained, score-targeted shared item subset and decoder predict retained full-benchmark accuracy and model rankings at 5-, 10-, and 25-item budgets, relative to local benchmark-estimation baselines, and where does the comparison reverse?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On nine frozen benchmark matrices, the evaluated score-targeted acquisition-and-decoding package has lower average accuracy-prediction error than the recorded baselines when averaging equally over benchmarks, seeds, and query budgets, but its advantage over local IRT is not uniform across benchmarks and does not establish the benefit of any individual decoder component or transfer beyond this snapshot.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation of one frozen package against paired, budget-matched local estimators, including an evaluator-provided lineage-block uncertainty analysis. The independent qualification sources report completed and qualified; no downstream_study is present. Aggregate candidate MAE is 0.038991605535293065, compared with local IRT 0.05911223215296765 and uniform sampling 0.10520541810011626. The IRT paired MAE-difference interval excludes zero conditional on the fixed cases, while the raw case-by-budget predictions reveal reversals in math, legalbench, and med_qa. These reversals are part of the thesis, not inconvenient supplementary-only results. Prefer one shared estimation question with condition-specific evidence blocks for the leaderboard and HELM matrices, not separate chronological experiments. Reject a random-audit unbiasedness paper because that is not the final evaluated package; reject a nonlinear-decoder superiority paper because activation and isolated component effects are unverified; reject a universal or adaptive-evaluation story because the evaluated item path is source-only and shared across targets. The empirical framing is ready, but the supplied bibliography needs task-relevant, permitted-source repair before a defensible final related-work section can be written.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"This is fresh framing, not an enabled manuscript-edit pass: no resolved edit-context file, previous_paper manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is supplied here. The recentered label describes narrowing the broad source-task brief to the frozen evaluated package and its heterogeneous accuracy, not a claim to have revised an unseen prior manuscript. Preserve-only framing has no prior text to justify; broadening to a shared adaptive or cross-setting method family is unsupported; recentering on the final paired evidence is strongest. Do not inherit a title or figure plan from private memory.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"paper_data.json.final_evaluation, read independently of the absent downstream_study, supplies nine distinct final cases, three seeds, three query budgets, paired baseline predictions, and qualification facts. It changes the center of gravity from broad method search to a frozen-package comparison.\",\n    \"Direct inspection of results/ideas/03_log_odds_shift_residual_decoder/program_1/src/program.py, surrogate.py, and nonlinear_decoder.py establishes a common score-covariance item path, all-query ridge/offset decoding, and a source-selected optional log-odds/residual branch with an incumbent fallback. The source tree is the local counterpart identified by the frozen selection manifest; no candidate was executed in this stage. No per-case selected branch is attested by the final evidence.\",\n    \"task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/*_manifest.json clarify group-weighted errors, lineage-mean ranking, equal-case aggregation, replay exclusion, local baseline definitions, content reconciliation, and metadata-limited lineage separation. They prevent claims about universally independent models or every original benchmark item.\",\n    \"Diagnostic postprocessing of final raw predictions, excluding replay and using lineage-equal weights, identifies local IRT reversals: math at all budgets, legalbench at 5 and 25, and med_qa at 10 and 25. These derived diagnostic signs determine the main figure plan; exact plotted values must be regenerated from the same records, not copied from rounded diagnostics.\",\n    \"No ablation studies or previously generated figures are supplied. The earlier estimator implementations are development-only context, not mandatory main-paper groups or controlled component ablations. No new experiments are prerequisites for this bounded comparison story.\",\n    \"VENUE.md and paper.sty require an anonymized ICLR 2026 single-column paper with 5.5-inch text width, 9-inch text height, and an 8-to-9-page body. The figure plan therefore uses full-text-width placement, not two-column assumptions.\",\n    \"references.bib and references_provenance.json contain mostly off-topic retrievals; reference_retrieval.log shows the quota was exhausted before the planned LLM-specific title queries. Existing metadata is not evidence that relevant literature was read. No retrieval, citation-network browsing, or prohibited-literature identification was attempted during framing.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Score-targeted acquisition and source-selected score decoding\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the frozen final package: lineage-weighted source response/score covariance, regularized conditional-gain selection with one representative per content group, a nested common subset, and all-observation decoding. Explain linear ridge/offset and optional log-odds plus residual ridge as implemented alternatives within the package; do not claim the optional branch was active or responsible for the measured gain.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Uniform content-group sampling\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Recorded, budget-matched simple comparator. The local implementation samples distinct content-group representatives, accounts for group mass, and clips its estimate; describe this rather than silently substituting an ordinary unweighted item mean.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Source-score prior\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Recorded no-target-response reference quantifies what can be predicted from source scores alone; its observation cost is zero even when displayed beside the other methods at each budget.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Response-vector anchors\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Recorded source-response representative selection and cluster-weighted averaging contextualize a structurally different acquisition/estimation family. The local normalized-response, farthest-first implementation is not an attested reproduction of the published Anchor Points method.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Local Rasch-style IRT estimator\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The strongest recorded aggregate comparator and the essential reference for heterogeneous failures. It uses source-fitted item difficulty, the same uniform query path as the uniform baseline, and queried-response target-ability fitting. It is not the complete tinyBenchmarks system or an exhaustive tuned IRT comparison.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Primary estimand: full mean binary accuracy over the retained, reconciled item matrix, not necessarily the original unfiltered benchmark. Primary error is target-lineage-equal MAE, then equally averaged across the declared budgets, seeds, and nine cases. Source and target model-lineage group assignments are disjoint under the supplied metadata policy, not under a proved complete ancestry relation.\",\n    \"Main aggregate table: selected package MAE 0.038991605535293065 and RMSE 0.04837892636321264; local IRT MAE 0.05911223215296765; uniform 0.10520541810011626; source prior 0.08335016635136305; anchors 0.15674229873896958. Ground every reported final metric in final.metric.* and keep raw score units in [0,1]. Do not imply these averages are specifically 25-item results.\",\n    \"Main paired uncertainty view: candidate-minus-IRT MAE -0.02012062661767458, recorded 95% interval [-0.02570984836073398, -0.014632649205711504]. Also show the recorded paired differences and intervals against uniform, prior, and anchors. The 200-draw lineage-block bootstrap retains seeds and budgets within groups and conditions on the fixed nine cases; the identical interval repeated in each seed wrapper is one aggregate analysis, not three independent intervals.\",\n    \"Main heterogeneity view: all nine cases at each budget, expressed as paired candidate-minus-local-IRT MAE with a common zero reference. Preserve math losses at every budget, legalbench reversals at 5 and 25, and med_qa reversals at 10 and 25. Do not infer causes from ecosystem membership, source count, or benchmark names. Any case-specific interval would require a documented new postprocessing analysis; the supplied aggregate interval cannot be reused as a per-case interval.\",\n    \"Ranking is a distinct outcome, not an inferred consequence of MAE: final Spearman 0.8158480130897703 and Kendall 0.6715966634525837 are calculated on lineage-mean predictions/truth and then averaged by the evaluator. Do not concatenate models from different benchmarks into one correlation. Baseline ranking cells may be filled only by a reproducible extraction from existing baseline predictions with exactly the same rules; otherwise show not reported, not zero, and make no ranking-superiority claim.\",\n    \"Robustness/control reporting: recorded permuted-source MAE 0.06257521756337783 and zero-response-control MAE 0.21577661527598374 concern interventions on the same package. Constant refers to zeroed target observations in the protocol, not necessarily a constant predictor; permuted refers to independent row-wise circular shifts of source columns. These are sensitivity checks, not isolated ablations or proof of the mechanism, validity, or absence of contamination.\",\n    \"Search and final are different evidence scopes: development uses arc_challenge, ifeval, commonsense, and gsm with seeds 101 and 211; final uses bbh, gpqa, mmlu_pro, musr, openllm_math, legalbench, math, med_qa, and mmlu with seeds 1009, 2003, and 3001. No pooled search/final table, before/after gain, cross-variant significance, search-score delta, or attempt-loss rate is planned. No study.* namespace exists here to support a downstream campaign accounting analysis.\",\n    \"Preserve original-comparator honesty: the initial anchor/ridge implementation is readable under task/program_0 but has no matched final measurements in this bundle; omit a quantitative original-program comparison rather than borrowing an incumbent score from memory. Retain the four actual recorded final comparators. No controlled ablation bundle exists.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: historical source responses and full source scores produce one common score-targeted item path; a new target contributes only queried answers to a source-selected decoder; complete retained target scores are used solely for external evaluation. Distinguish source fitting, legal target observation, and scoring by labeled lanes and a visual information boundary. Mark optional nonlinear decoding as source-selected, not as an experimentally established mechanism.\",\n    \"Figure 2, aggregate paired comparison: a horizontal forest plot of candidate-minus-baseline MAE for the four recorded baselines, using the one supplied aggregate lineage-block interval per comparator and a zero line. A nearby compact table carries absolute errors and separately identified ranking results.\",\n    \"Figure 3, scope and reversals: two vertically stacked dot-plot panels, one with the five leaderboard cases and one with the four HELM cases; within each case show the three budget-specific candidate-minus-local-IRT MAEs using displaced symbols, not connected progression curves. A common signed x-axis exposes local losses as well as gains.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"score_prediction_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the estimation target, shared score-directed subset, source-selected decoder, and target-truth information boundary legible at a glance.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_role\",\n      \"metric\": \"none_conceptual_information_flow\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected paper.sty sets one 5.5-inch-wide column and 9-inch text height. Use the full 5.5-inch text width, about 2.0 inches of drawing height plus a short caption, early on page 2; a half-width figure would make matrix labels and the training/evaluation boundary too small. Allocate about 0.30 body page including caption. Use one panel, at most six labeled blocks, and at least 9-point rendered labels.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"paired_error_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show the signed aggregate error difference and conditional uncertainty against every recorded baseline without equating average gains with universal wins.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"forest_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"baseline_method\",\n      \"metric\": \"candidate_minus_baseline_mae_with_recorded_95_percent_interval\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none_equal_case_budget_seed_aggregate\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the single-column 5.5-inch text width and about 1.7 inches of axes height, with four comparator rows and one point/interval per row. The full span preserves readable comparator names, signed ticks, and a zero reference at 9-point or larger type. Place near the aggregate results, around page 6; budget about 0.26 body page including caption. Do not squeeze it into a hypothetical two-column slot or truncate interval endpoints.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"case_budget_reversals\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose budget-specific benchmark heterogeneity, including where the selected package loses to the strongest recorded aggregate baseline.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"faceted_horizontal_dot_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"benchmark\",\n      \"metric\": \"candidate_minus_local_irt_mae\",\n      \"split_by\": \"data_ecosystem_with_budget_symbols\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"At the inspected 5.5-inch single-column width, stack the five-case and four-case panels vertically in one column rather than shrinking nine case labels into adjacent mini-panels. Target about 3.2 inches total axes height, one shared signed x-scale, three offset symbols per case for 5/10/25 items, and labels at least 9 points. Place around page 7 and budget about 0.43 body page including caption. Use labels or shapes as well as color; never suppress positive differences or connect points as a search trajectory.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"Supply reimplementation, auditability, and complete evidence disaggregation after the references while keeping aggregate uncertainty, benchmark reversals, and the principal limitations in the main paper; no proposed experiment is presented as completed.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Weighted Acquisition and Decoder Specification\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Expand the weighted covariance definitions, conditional squared score-covariance over regularized conditional item variance, content-group exclusion, ridge/offset prediction, and optional penalized log-odds/full-population curve/residual-ridge equations. Transcribe the implemented tuning shortlists, fold construction, clipping, zero-budget fallback, and insufficient-group/allowance fallback from the inspected selected src/surrogate.py and src/nonlinear_decoder.py. Treat component behavior as code specification, not measured ablation evidence. The final evidence and frozen tree metadata link this specification to the studied package.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.qualification.status\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Frozen Data and Lineage/Content Provenance\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the pinned benchmark-prediction snapshot, task/resources/rebuild.py, retained columns, source/target row and item-group policies, and file hashes in task/resources/final/*_manifest.json. Reconcile dimensions with the evaluated arrays rather than generic counts from the task brief. Include metadata-limited ancestry and conservative common-stem limitations; these also receive a concise main-text disclosure. Do not expose model identities or file paths as a substitute for scientific grouping definitions.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Benchmark, Budget, and Seed Results\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate every non-replay normal case/budget/seed result from final raw predictions for the selected package and four recorded baseline prediction arrays. Compute lineage-equal MAE and the evaluator-defined ranking summaries reproducibly, reconcile the primary aggregates to final.metric.*, and retain all loss cells. Identify any newly derived baseline RMSE or ranking statistics as postprocessing of existing predictions, not new experimental runs. Do not invent intervals for individual cells.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.mae\", \"final.metric.rmse\", \"final.metric.spearman\", \"final.metric.kendall\", \"final.metric.irt_mae\", \"final.metric.uniform_mae\", \"final.metric.anchor_mae\", \"final.metric.prior_mae\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Paired Uncertainty and Observation Controls\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Explain the single recorded 200-draw, fixed-case, lineage-block bootstrap, retention of checkpoints/seeds within groups, equal-case averaging, and treatment of missing cells in the evaluator policy. Report the actual recorded zero failed normal cells, without converting it into a performance success rate. Expand normal, zeroed-target-observation, source-column-shift, duplicate-history, and deterministic-replay definitions from task/evaluate_final.py. Keep control behavior distinct from full validity certification and do not count the BBH replay as another benchmark.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.constant_mae\", \"final.metric.permuted_mae\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.status\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Reproduction and Evidence Boundaries\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Provide anonymized data reconstruction and evaluation commands based on the inspected scripts, available requirement/specification files, artifact hashes, exact recorded seeds and budgets, and a table mapping each final table/figure to its eventual extraction script and source records. Commands not rerun by the writer must be labeled as such. Describe CPU requirements as requirements, not measured speedups; give no unmeasured end-to-end runtime. Preserve final completed/qualified outcome separately from search feedback, and record the absence of controlled ablations and per-case decoder-selection diagnostics.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the documented use of LLM agents for research ideation, candidate implementation, reference-query planning, paper framing, and eventual writing/figure assistance, distinguishing these roles from evaluator-produced measurements. Identify verification actually performed rather than inventing human review. Document that framing performed local artifact inspection and prediction postprocessing only, not candidate execution or additional experiments. Track any later literature incident truthfully without guessing the prohibited work's identity.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Phases\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"First Valid Result\",\n    \"Iteration History\",\n    \"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\n    \"From Audit to Ridge to Nonlinear\",\n    \"Winning Configuration\",\n    \"Nonlinear Decoder Ablation\",\n    \"Universal Benchmark Prediction\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\",\n    \"state of the art\",\n    \"wins on every benchmark\",\n    \"proves generalization\",\n    \"leakage-free\",\n    \"the nonlinear component caused the gain\",\n    \"unbiased rankings\",\n    \"adaptive per-target acquisition\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Plan an approximately 8.5-page ICLR 2026 body including figures/tables, excluding references and substantive Supplementary Information. Allocate 1.5 pages to title/abstract/introduction plus the early overview; 1.0 to problem/estimand and focused related work; 1.8 to acquisition, decoding and source-only tuning; 1.1 to frozen protocol/data/baseline definitions; 2.0 to aggregate uncertainty, ranking and case/budget reversals; and 1.1 to limitations, interpretation and conclusion. If the body ends at eight pages, the final body page must be at least 75% full; never exceed nine body pages. Use available space for equations, definitions and evidence interpretation, not padding or a run diary.\",\n    \"Suggested thesis-first headings: Introduction; Problem and Related Work; Score-Targeted Subset Prediction; Evaluation Protocol; Aggregate Accuracy and Conditional Uncertainty; Benchmark-Specific Reversals and Ranking; Scope and Limitations; Conclusion. Results subsections may share one top-level Results heading. Put substantive Supplementary Information after references; include a separate LLM Usage section there.\",\n    \"Qualification is independently grounded in final.qualification.status=completed, final.qualification.outcome=qualified, and final.qualification.correct=true. Do not derive qualification from a search score, remembered acceptance statement, or publication readiness. Do not claim unseen gate diagnostics establish adaptivity, generalization, or complete validity. If a later authoritative evaluation changes the outcome, preserve that outcome and any reported limitations throughout writing, review, audit, and revision; missing causes stay unknown.\",\n    \"Use final.metric.* for final numbers and final.evidence.episodes for paired analyses. There are no study.* sources and no downstream_study; omit search-score differences and scored/unscored accounting rather than reconstructing them from private memory. Never treat an unscored attempt as a measured loss. Report no inherited original-program metric without matched evidence.\",\n    \"Private-only artifact identifiers, hashes, result.* search fields, and internal selection labels belong in evidence bookkeeping, not titles, abstract, tables, or visible metrics. Refer to the frozen package, model variants, query budgets, and recorded evaluation. Source identifiers listed in this strategy are audit anchors, not literal paper prose.\",\n    \"All figure spans refer to the actual one-column 5.5-inch text width in paper.sty: column width and full text width coincide here. Do not use figure* or 0.48-width two-column defaults. Respect the per-figure height, label and panel budgets; at most two total panels are needed and no figure has more than one panel per row. Prefer a page-2 overview to adding a dense late appendix architecture diagram.\",\n    \"Only score_prediction_overview uses generate-concept-figure; the downstream orchestrator owns that pipeline, and this stage only plans it. Require an editable diagram source and deterministic faithful export; do not fabricate illustrative outcome values or imply active nonlinear decoding. Quantitative figures use source-controlled matplotlib extraction/drawing with exact final records.\",\n    \"Require PDF and PNG exports from source for quantitative figures, PNG at 300 DPI, and use the PDF in LaTeX whenever available. Preserve vector diagram exports when available. Raster-only full-width output must be at least 2400 pixels wide; column/half-width raster-only output, if ever justified, must be at least 1200 pixels wide. A 5.5-inch 300-DPI preview is not sufficient for raster-only full-width placement; regenerate at the required source size, never upscale.\",\n    \"The baseline table has five method rows, clear observation costs, MAE for all methods, and separately labeled selected-package RMSE/ranking if other statistics are not yet derived. Missing metrics are marked not reported, not inferred. Include the aggregate IRT paired interval, all principal reversals, lack of component attribution, metadata-limited ancestry, and fixed-snapshot uncertainty in main text, not only the supplement.\",\n    \"Do not add an algorithm-comparison ablation table: ablation_studies is empty. The optional nonlinear decoder retains a linear fallback, and candidate diagnostics are marked unverified. Code availability establishes the algorithm family, not that the nonlinear branch improved a particular benchmark. Component attribution and broad source-to-target transfer remain open questions, not completed experiments.\",\n    \"The response-vector anchor and local IRT baselines are local implementations, not published-method leaderboard entries. Uniform and IRT share a uniform query path, whereas the proposed package also changes acquisition; paired errors compare packages at matching item budgets, not isolated decoders, matched training compute, or a globally optimal tuning budget.\",\n    \"Before related-work prose, repair the relevance/readership gap in the supplied bibliography using only explicitly permitted direct-title sources from the task brief or focused unrelated foundational work. Current entries such as Derezinski2019BayesianED, Ravi2016ExperimentalDO and Uto2020AGM are possible contextual leads only, not proof of a new connection or a substitute for read task literature. Do not cite unrelated materials merely to reach a reference count, do not invent BibTeX keys, and do not claim metadata-only entries were read. This stage changes neither bibliography nor provenance and conducts no retrieval.\",\n    \"Never identify, search for, infer, or follow citations toward the prohibited existing study. Use only permitted direct-source literature without citation-network expansion; if an accidental encounter occurs later, stop and document the location and reading extent. No such encounter was identified during this local-only framing stage.\",\n    \"Anonymize ICLR submission text: omit authors, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, personal links and internal workspace URLs. Scientific limitations and precise LLM-use disclosure must survive all later review and revision. Do not claim a reviewer checklist was required when the supplied venue/style does not establish that requirement.\",\n    \"This is a strategy-only deliverable: do not create paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, new experiments, figures, or final validation artifacts here. Check JSON shape, memo agreement, actual source-ID resolution, qualification preservation and layout/readability budgets only. Later authoritative analysis rankings route appendix material to Supplementary Information without retroactively rewriting this strategy.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Frozen Surrogate Random Audit; required JSON bundles written; status=idea_bundle_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Only an untested proposal and interface evidence are available; no evaluated scientific finding warrants distilled guidance changes.\n  - evidence: Supplied manifest: incumbent correct=true, combined_score=0.9080607409041159. Named incumbent source file unavailable. Mounted README documents repeated identities and shared details_repo values, but no group assignments. No candidate, benchmark, optimizer, evaluator, or computational probe executed. Alternatives: source-only IRT/anchors, supervised selection, pure random averaging; selected frozen-surrogate residual correction has a conditional finite-population argument but unknown empirical benefit and possible audit-variance/pilot-capacity costs.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:54:24.868797+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.9189386797038666, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"anchor_mae\": 0.13300734976798292, \"combined_score\": 0.9189386797038666, \"constant_mae\": 0.4580913999000772, \"control_correct\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"irt_mae\": 0.07504423299125063, \"kendall\": 0.6250942381270175, \"mae\": 0.0810613202961334, \"permuted_mae\": 0.08687585668671148, \"prior_mae\": 0.14429218883447575, \"rmse\": 0.10282682720675318, \"spearman\": 0.7816459431057419, \"uniform_mae\": 0.11168295368474038}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Score Targeted All Query Ridge; required JSON bundles written; status=idea_bundle_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=One aggregate evaluation motivates a new experiment but supplies neither uncertainty nor evidence of its mechanism or transfer; no stable distilled recommendation is established.\n  - evidence: Recorded incumbent correct=true, combined_score=0.9189386797038666, MAE=0.0810613202961334; uniform MAE=0.11168295368474038; IRT MAE=0.07504423299125063. Candidate-owned notes document a fixed initial pilot count. Criterion-level rubric failure cause is unknown. Alternatives considered: unsupported per-target adaptive IRT, nonlinear source-response mixtures and audit-allocation repair. Selected score-directed conditional covariance acquisition and all-answer ridge decoding are supported by the common-subset RPC, supplied source weights and anonymous source/content groups. No new scientific computation or evaluator access occurred.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final comparison: MAE 3.90 pp, RMSE 4.84 pp, Spearman 0.816, Kendall 0.672 for the frozen package; baseline MAEs IRT 5.91, uniform 10.52, prior 8.34, anchors 15.67 pp.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.mae\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse\",\n        \"final.metric.spearman\",\n        \"final.metric.kendall\",\n        \"final.metric.irt_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.uniform_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.prior_mae\",\n        \"final.metric.anchor_mae\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Nine final matrices, seeds 1009/2003/3001, budgets 5/10/25; 81 non-replay normal cells. Normal BBH replay excluded. Final and development results remain separate.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"analysis/paper_statistics.json#/cell_count\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Development cases ARC-Challenge, IFEval, Commonsense, and GSM are not pooled into final results.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.top_program_01\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Final evaluation completed and qualified; no failed normal cells recorded. This does not establish generality or an unrecorded gate diagnosis.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_refs\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation\",\n        \"analysis/paper_statistics.json#/uncertainty\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Table 1 equal-cell aggregate, selected: {\\\"mae\\\": 0.03899160553529307, \\\"rmse\\\": 0.04837892636321264, \\\"spearman\\\": 0.8158480130897703, \\\"kendall\\\": 0.6715966634525838}\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.mae\",\n        \"final.metric.rmse\",\n        \"final.metric.spearman\",\n        \"final.metric.kendall\"\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:16:30.449847+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2309.08638\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2402.14992\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2407.12844\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2409.03563\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2410.05952\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2601.13885\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2406.01574\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2211.09110\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2412.06745\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2210.09261\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2311.12022\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2310.16049\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2103.03874\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2009.13081\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2009.03300\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2308.11462\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 1506.02629\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2006.10256\",\n    \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 1907.10121\",\n    \"Direct foundational DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1970.10488634\",\n    \"Direct foundational DOI: 10.1214/aoms/1177729442\",\n    \"Direct foundational DOI: 10.1214/aos/1176344552\",\n    \"Direct foundational DOI: 10.1080/01621459.1987.10478410\",\n    \"Direct foundational DOI: 10.1109/MCSE.2007.55\",\n    \"Direct known foundational article\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2309.08638\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"arXiv:2309.08638\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Vivek2024\",\n      \"title\": \"Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Vivek, Rajan\",\n        \"Ethayarajh, Kawin\",\n        \"Yang, Diyi\",\n        \"Kiela, Douwe\"\n      ],\n      \"year\": 2023,\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.08638\",\n      \"query\": \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2309.08638\",\n      \"abstract\": \"Abstract: Modern language models often exhibit powerful but brittle behavior, leading to the development of larger and more diverse benchmarks to reliably assess their behavior. Here, we suggest that model performance can be benchmarked and elucidated with much smaller evaluation sets. We first show that in six popular language classification benchmarks, model confidence in the correct class on many pairs of points is strongly correlated across models. We build upon this phenomenon to propose Anchor Point Selection, a technique to select small subsets of datasets that capture model behavior across the entire dataset. Anchor points reliably rank models: across 87 diverse language model-prompt pairs, evaluating models using 1-30 anchor points outperforms uniform sampling and other baselines at accurately ranking models. Moreover, just several anchor points can be used to estimate model per-class predictions on all other points in a dataset with low mean absolute error, sufficient for gauging where the model is likely to fail. Lastly, we present Anchor Point Maps for visualizing these insights and facilitating comparisons of the performance of different models on various regions within the dataset distribution.\",\n      \"consulted_path\": \"literature_evidence/Vivek2024.html\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"abstract and bibliographic metadata\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:16:30.449847+00:00\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2402.14992\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"arXiv:2402.14992\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Polo2024\",\n      \"title\": \"tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\",\n      \"authors\": [\n        \"Polo, Felipe Maia\",\n        \"Weber, Lucas\",\n        \"Choshen, Leshem\",\n        \"Sun, Yuekai\",\n        \"Xu, Gongjun\",\n        \"Yurochkin, Mikhail\"\n      ],\n      \"year\": 2024,\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14992\",\n      \"query\": \"Direct supplied or foundational identifier: 2402.14992\",\n      \"abstract\": \"Abstract: The versatility of large language models (LLMs) led to the creation of diverse benchmarks that thoroughly test a variety of language models' abilities. These benchmarks consist of tens of thousands of examples making evaluation of LLMs very expensive. In this paper, we investigate strategies to reduce the number of evaluations needed to assess the performance of an LLM on several key benchmarks. For example, we show that to accurately estimate the performance of an LLM on MMLU, a popular multiple-choice QA benchmark consisting of 14K examples, it is sufficient to evaluate this LLM on 100 curated examples. We release evaluation tools and tiny versions of popular benchmarks: Open LLM Leaderboard, MMLU, HELM, and AlpacaEval 2.0. Our empirical analysis demonstrates that these tools and tiny benchmarks are sufficient to reliably and efficiently reproduce the original evaluation results.\",\n      \"consulted_path\": \"literature_evidence/Polo2024.html\",\n      \"consultation_scope\": \"abstract and bibliographic metadata\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:16:30.449847+00:00\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
